---
/**
 * Research overview.
 *
 * NOTE: the opening paragraph and the first paragraph of "Counting every
 * rotation" are Jackson's own words. Claude.md rule 3 covers them: fact-check
 * only, never rewrite or restyle.
 *
 * The remaining prose is still placeholder, written only from the published
 * abstracts so the page does not sit empty. It makes no claim that is not in
 * one of the six papers. Jackson should replace it with his own account of the
 * work; those sections are still marked with a TODO.
 */
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const research = (await getEntry('site', 'research'))!.data;
const interests: string[] = research.interests ?? [];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research"
  description="Pulsar timing, millisecond pulsars in binaries, and searches for unseen companions."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <h1>Research</h1>
      {
        interests.length > 0 && (
          <p class="interests">
            {interests.map((item: string, i: number) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 ? <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span> : ''}
                <span>{item}</span>
              </>
            ))}
          </p>
        )
      }
    </div>

    {/* Jackson's own words. Claude.md rule 3: do not rewrite or restyle. */}
    <p class="opening">
      I work with millisecond pulsars, which are pulsars that spin at up to 716 times per second.
      In particular, I study what we can learn about when their
      pulses arrive on earth, known as "times of arrival."
    </p>

    <section class="topic" aria-labelledby="t-timing">
      <h2 id="t-timing">Counting every rotation</h2>
      {/* First paragraph is Jackson's own words. Claude.md rule 3: do not rewrite or restyle. */}
      <p>
        Pulsar timing accounts for every rotation of a pulsar between observations. By doing so, we
        can learn about how fast the pulsar is rotating, its position in the sky, the nature of how
        it orbits another star, and even properties of the Milky Way galaxy. Getting the count right
        normally takes tightly scheduled monitoring and a good deal of practice. I wrote an
        algorithm that does the phase connection for binary pulsars on its own, using the
        statistical F-test and the quadratic behaviour of the reduced χ² near a minimum.
      </p>
      <p>
        It produced timing solutions for two newly discovered binary pulsars in
        the globular cluster Terzan 5, from about 70 Green Bank Telescope observations spanning 13
        years. A run can take hours. That is less of a problem than it sounds:
      </p>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          This may seem like a long runtime, but a human would likely take much longer and give up
          from frustration after many incorrect attempts.
        </p>
        <cite>Algorithmic Pulsar Timer for Binaries, 2024</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/publications/algorithmic-pulsar-timer/">Algorithmic Pulsar Timer for Binaries</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* TODO: replace with Jackson's own wording */}
    <section class="topic" aria-labelledby="t-trojans">
      <h2 id="t-trojans">What else might be in the orbit</h2>
      <p>
        Trojan asteroids sit at the equilateral Lagrange points of the Sun–Jupiter system in great
        number. Trojans of up to planetary mass have been predicted in other systems, and none has
        been confirmed. I took that search to radio pulsars with very low-mass companions, where
        pulse arrival times are measured well enough to register the reflex motion a librating
        Trojan would cause. Eight pulsars from the NANOGrav 15 yr dataset gave upper limits near one
        Earth mass. Two of them show weak signals consistent with a few Jupiter masses, which most
        likely have some other source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Why the answer might be no is a separate question, and the paper offers a few
        possibilities. This is the one I like best:
      </p>
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          Mass transfer and disk dynamics in pulsar binary systems are clearly turbulent, and so,
          any interloping Trojan may find itself ejected from the system or torn apart.
        </p>
        <cite>Searching for Exotrojans in Pulsar Systems, 2026</cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/publications/exotrojans/">Searching for Exotrojans in Pulsar Systems</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* TODO: replace with Jackson's own wording */}
    <section class="topic" aria-labelledby="t-ffo">
      <h2 id="t-ffo">Objects bound to nothing</h2>
      <p>
        Rogue planets, brown dwarfs and large asteroids that belong to no star are expected
        throughout the Milky Way, and three interstellar objects have already been caught passing
        through the solar system on hyperbolic orbits. If one of them goes close enough to a
        millisecond pulsar, its gravity shifts the pulse arrival times by an amount that can be
        measured. We searched the NANOGrav 15 yr narrowband dataset, 68 pulsars in all, for those
        encounters. Nothing turned up at statistical significance, and the non-detection sets upper
        limits on the number density of such objects nearby. They are the first limits of that kind
        from pulsar timing data.
      </p>
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/publications/free-floating-objects/">
          Search for Gravitational Scattering of Pulsars by Free-floating Objects
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* TODO: replace with Jackson's own wording */}
    <section class="topic" aria-labelledby="t-discovery">
      <h2 id="t-discovery">Finding new pulsars</h2>
      <p>
        Some of the work is discovery. A targeted search with the Green Bank Telescope for radio
        pulsations from a redback candidate turned up the 1.68 ms binary pulsar
        <span class="mono">PSR J2053+6905</span>, and multi-epoch optical photometry and
        spectroscopy of the same system constrain the two component masses. The CHIME fast radio
        burst instrument found <span class="mono">PSR J2108+5055</span>, a sporadic mode-changing
        source whose brightest pulse reached a flux density of 3.1 kJy. MeerKAT observations of
        Terzan 5 produced ten more millisecond pulsars, bringing the count for that cluster to 49.
      </p>
      <p class="more">
        <a href="/publications/redback-j2053/">Redback pulsar PSR J2053+6905</a>
        <a href="/publications/sporadic-radio-pulsar/">An extremely luminous sporadic radio pulsar</a>
        <a href="/publications/terzan5-ten-pulsars/">Ten new millisecond pulsars in Terzan 5</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .interests {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .opening {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .topic {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .topic h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }

  .topic p + p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .quote {
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 1.1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
  }

  .quote p {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .quote cite {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.45rem;
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .more {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
